From 9fd6077281fae6bfe8bac349008cc2f5e6c69221 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 23:29:13 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] settings: reset the engine when the transport type changes

ptp_set_transport_type() wrote the new transport into the profile and stopped
there, while its immediate neighbour does the necessary second half and says
why:

    void ptp_set_transport_type(PtpTransportType tp) {
        S.profile.transportType = tp;
    }

    void ptp_set_delay_mechanism(PtpDelayMechanism dm) {
        S.profile.delayMechanism = dm;
        ptp_reset(); // this is mandatory
    }

It is equally mandatory here. The network stack driver latches the transport
in its own state at ptp_nsd_init() time -- nsd_lwip.c keeps it in a static TP,
and the other drivers do the same -- and nothing else re-runs that. Without a
reset the core believes one thing and the driver another: `ptp transport
802.3` prints "Transport layer: 802.3" and `ptp info` agrees, while the driver
carries on with UDP sockets and never emits an 802.3 frame.

Measured on a NUCLEO-H563ZI running the FreeRTOS + lwIP demo, counting
raw-802.3 PTP frames transmitted after `ptp profile preset defp2p` followed by
`ptp transport 802.3`, over 100 s:

    before:    0 frames
    after:   280 frames

Zero, from a cold boot as well, so it is not a stale-state artefact. The gPTP
preset was unaffected and hid this, because its own transportType is already
802.3 and the profile load resets the engine anyway -- the defect only shows
when the transport is changed on its own.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/settings_interface.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/settings_interface.c b/src/flexptp/settings_interface.c
index f87ebed..322c273 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/settings_interface.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/settings_interface.c
@@ -70,6 +70,7 @@ PtpTransportType ptp_get_transport_type() {
 
 void ptp_set_transport_type(PtpTransportType tp) {
     S.profile.transportType = tp;
+    ptp_reset(); // this is mandatory, exactly as it is for the delay mechanism below
 }
 
 void ptp_set_delay_mechanism(PtpDelayMechanism dm) {
